## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `RunLoop` `while(true)` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| **Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns index: 0 = new event, 1 = cancellation
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
            if (signaled == 1)
                break; // cancellation requested

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string item;
            lock (_lock) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); }
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation
        // guarantees the loop exits at the next WaitAny call
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — The token's `WaitHandle` is included in `WaitHandle.WaitAny`, so cancellation wakes the blocked thread instantly. No need for `Interrupt`.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → removed** — Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+. The cooperative loop check (`IsCancellationRequested` + `WaitAny`) guarantees the thread exits, so the forcible fallback is unnecessary.

3. **`WaitOne()` → `WaitAny(handles)`** — The original `WaitOne` couldn't respond to cancellation. `WaitAny` with two handles unblocks on *either* a new event or cancellation.

4. **`IDisposable` added** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed. The original code leaked the `ManualResetEvent` too.

## Pitfalls to Watch For

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Race between `Dequeue` and cancellation** | If cancelled right after `WaitAny` returns 0, the queue item is still processed. This is correct — you don't lose in-flight work. If you want to drain remaining items on shutdown, add a drain loop after the `while` exits. |
| **Multiple items enqueued before `WaitAny` returns** | The original code had this same bug — `Reset()` + single `Dequeue()` drops items when multiple `Enqueue` calls fire before the loop iterates. Fix: drain all items per wakeup (see below). |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Causes timer and callback leaks. Always wrap in `IDisposable` or `using`. |
| **`Thread.Interrupt` is also obsolete** | Marked `SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9. Even if targeting .NET 8 today, replace it now to avoid a second migration. |

## Bonus: Fix the Pre-existing Multi-Enqueue Bug

```csharp
private void RunLoop()
{
    var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

    while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
        if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
            break;

        _newEvent.Reset();

        // Drain all queued items per wakeup
        while (true)
        {
            string item;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count == 0) break;
                item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }
            Handle(item);
        }
    }
}
```

This ensures no items are silently lost when multiple `Enqueue` calls happen between loop iterations.
